Node(Entity) coding rules:
You must extract all nodes as comprehensively as possible and normalize all node strings strictly to one of the following 9 categories. Each node corresponds to only one category; a category usually contains multiple nodes. Use the provided boundary rules to resolve classification ambiguities:
1. `Explicit Knowledge`: Specific piece of codified information (e.g., a manual, a database, a written report, rule-based standard metrics). Keep the node names consistent with the terms used in the paper.
2. `Tacit Knowledge`: Specific uncodified skill, intuition, routine, safe behavioral response, self-efficacy belief, or "know-how" held by an individual (e.g., real-time hazard identification performance, situational awareness).
3. `Knowledge Holder`: Specific role, expert, or team possessing all or part of the knowledge (e.g., "Senior Engineer", not "Engineers").Keep the node names consistent with the terms used in the paper.
4. `Traditional Human-central Practice`: Specific non-digital, human-driven mechanism for transfer (e.g., "Exit interview", "Shadowing session", text manual self-study).
5. `Technology-enable Practice`: Specific software, hardware, or digital system actively used to host or capture knowledge (e.g., "Wiki page", "Knowledge graph", interactive immersive simulation).
6. `Digital Technology`: Specific emerging digital technologies adopted by Technology-enable Practice. Keep the node names consistent with the terms used in the paper.
7. `Organizational Dependency`: Specific internal prerequisite or baseline condition required for the practice to succeed (e.g., "Corporate culture", "Dedicated training hours", "Management buy-in").
8. `Environmental Dependency`: Specific external factor outside the organization's direct control required for the practice (e.g., "Industry regulation", "Physical location constraint", "Internet infrastructure").
9. `Limitation`: Specific inherent vulnerability, shortcoming, or failure point of a practice itself (e.g., "Cognitive overload", "Data decay", "Loss of nuance", "Knowledge-to-practice gap", "Forgetting curve").

Relation type coding rules：
You must normalize all edge strings strictly to the following permitted semantic definitions. Ensure the edge direction strictly flows from `src_node_id` to `dst_node_id` using these exact singular terms:
* 'be_documented_partially_by': Links [Tacit Knowledge] ->to ->[Knowledge Holder] (Use this when knowledge holder codify human experience, routines, or behaviors  into structured text, files, or manuals.)* 'be_derived_from': Links [Explicit Knowledge] ->to ->[Explicit Knowledge] (Use this to trace the origin or lineage of structured documents, showing that one file or rule was sourced directly from another standard text.)
* 'translate_into': Links [Explicit Knowledge] ->to ->[Tacit Knowledge] (Use this when written rules, metrics, or guidelines are successfully put into practice by a person and turned into safe personal routines or hands-on skills.)
* 'be_held_by': Links [Tacit Knowledge OR Explicit Knowledge] ->to ->[Knowledge Holder] (Use this to show the baseline location of an asset, identifying which specific person, expert, or team currently possesses that know-how or documentation.)
* 'be_absorbed_by': Links [Tacit Knowledge OR Explicit Knowledge] ->to ->[Knowledge Holder] (Use this to mark a clear learning milestone, showing that a specific person or role has successfully gained new knowledge as a result of training.)
* 'be_captured_by': Links [Tacit Knowledge] ->to ->[Technology-enable Practice] (Use this when human routines, actions, or real-time spatial behaviors are actively tracked or monitored inside a digital application or simulation.)
* 'be_transferred_by': Links [Tacit Knowledge OR Explicit Knowledge] ->to ->[Traditional Human-central Practice OR Technology-enable Practice] (Use this to connect a specific knowledge asset to the exact training vehicle, manual review, or simulation system used to distribute it.)
* 'participate_in': Links [Knowledge Holder] ->to ->[Traditional Human-central Practice OR Technology-enable Practice] (Use this to map human actors to an operational workflow, showing who actively takes part in a chosen training approach or experiment.)
* 'depend_on': Links [Traditional Human-central Practice OR Technology-enable Practice] ->to ->[Organizational Dependency OR Environmental Dependency] (Use this to flag necessary baseline resources, company cultures, or external regulatory requirements needed for a practice to run successfully.)
* 'evaluate': Links [Technology-enable Practice OR Traditional Human-central Practice] ->to ->[Tacit Knowledge OR Explicit Knowledge] (Use this when a practice includes testing parameters, quizzes, or built-in performance scores designed to actively check a participant's skill or compliance level.)
* 'adopt': Links [Technology-enable Practice] ->to ->[Digital Technology OR Explicit Knowledge] (Use this to outline system architecture, connecting a digital practice to its underlying physical hardware tools, software engines, or structural data files.)
* 'be_constrained_by': Links [Traditional Human-central Practice OR Technology-enable Practice] ->to ->[Limitation] (Use this to tie a practice directly to its known shortcomings, system bottlenecks, or real-world flaws, like steep human forgetting or passivity.)
* 'resolve': Links [Technology-enable Practice] ->to $\rightarrow[Limitation] (Use this only when verified data proves that a digital or automated approach completely removes or neutralizes an existing process bottleneck.)
* 'mitigate': Links [Technology-enable Practice OR Traditional Human-central Practice] ->to ->[Tacit Knowledge OR Limitation] (Use this when an approach successfully lowers, softens, or controls a structural flaw or behavioral risk without completely eliminating it.)
* 'complement': Links [Technology-enable Practice] ->to ->[Traditional Human-central Practice] (Use this to show that a newly introduced digital framework enhances or patches a legacy human routine without making it obsolete.)
* 'cannot_fully_replace': Links [Technology-enable Practice] ->to ->[Traditional Human-central Practice] (Use this when case evidence proves that an automated tool still relies on human baselines, or that a physical practice remains legally mandatory.)
* 'be_difficult_to_capture_due_to': Links [Tacit Knowledge] ->to ->[Limitation] (Use this to map specific knowledge management roadblocks, explaining exactly what cognitive or process barriers prevent hands-on skills from being easily stored.)

